# Hand-off Rules for Development Sessions

1. Overview

This guideline standardizes how session progress and handoff logs are recorded for collaborative or agent-assisted development. It ensures clarity, traceability, and easy resumption across any programming language, platform, or automation framework.

⸻

2. Log File Structure
   • Directory: All handoff logs live in docs/handoffs/.
   • Filename: <ProjectName>\_Handoff-YYYYMMDD-HHMM.md
   • Example: MyAgentWorkflow_Handoff-20250722-1815.md
   • Uses an ISO 8601 timestamp for chronological sorting and collision avoidance.
   • Encoding: UTF-8, LF line endings.

⸻

3. Section Layout

Each markdown file must include these sections in order: 1. # [ProjectName] Handoff Log 2. ## Brief Context
• One to two sentences describing the session’s goal or focus. 3. ## Completed Work
• Bullet list of concrete actions taken, code written, or analyses performed. 4. ## Current State
• Short description of the system/code/data as of the handoff. 5. ## Next Steps
• Prioritized, actionable list (supports hierarchical numbering: 1., 1.1, 1.2, 2., etc.). 6. ## References
• File paths, URLs, code snippets, or other resources. 7. ## Notes (optional)
• Edge cases, blockers, or user/agent preferences.

⸻

4. Naming & Numbering Conventions
   • Filename always includes the project name and exact creation timestamp.
   • Section headings in English for cross-agent compatibility (content may be in any language).
   • Hierarchy:
   • Top-level: 1., 2., 3., …
   • Sub-steps: 1.1, 1.2, 2.1, …
   • Code blocks use fenced syntax with language tag:

# your code here

⸻

5. Automation Triggers
   • When to log:
   • Immediately before switching tasks or agents.
   • Upon explicit “handoff” command or when context-window usage (e.g., >50%) is reached.
   • What to do:
   • Auto-generate and commit a new markdown file following this template.
   • Populate filename and all sections programmatically.

⸻

6. Example

# MyAgentWorkflow Handoff Log

## Brief Context

- Focused on refactoring the input-parsing module and improving error handling.

## Completed Work

- Enhanced `normalize_input()` for Unicode normalization.
- Added retry logic (max_retries=3) on network failures.
- Wrote and passed 5 new unit tests.

## Current State

- Input-parsing module functions correctly, including retry behavior.

## Next Steps

1. Integrate external API  
   1.1. Implement OAuth authentication  
   1.2. Handle rate limiting
2. Standardize log output formatting by level
3. Add linting and tests to CI pipeline

## References

- `src/parsing/input_handler.py`
- Tests: `tests/test_input_handler.py`

## Notes

- Error messages are currently mixed Korean/English; decide on single language.
